<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Modibbo Adama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Modibbo Adama was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholar who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adamawa-emirate">Adamawa Emirate</a>, the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. Around 1806 or 1809 he received a flag from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> to spread the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> in Fombina, the southern lands, and conquered or absorbed many peoples across what are now north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> his capital, and the emirate was named after him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…dan Fodio led the campaigns against Gobir, while flagbearers such as Sulaimanu in Kano, Musa in Zaria and <b>Modibbo Adama</b> in Adamawa carved out new emirates; within some five years virtually every major Hausa ruler had been overthrown or subordinated, creating a federation of emirates acknowledging the spiritual…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Modibbo Adama</b>, a Fulani scholar, received a flag from Usman dan Fodio, traditionally in 1806 or 1809, to spread the jihad in Fombina, the southern lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/adamawa-emirate">Adamawa Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
